&lt;p&gt;The mathematical operation known as &lt;strong&gt;hyperexponentiation&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;tetration&lt;/strong&gt; of a number $a$ by a positive integer $b$ is symbolized as $a\mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow}b$ or $^b a$. It is defined recursively as follows:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
$a \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} 1 = a$,&lt;br&gt;
$a \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} (k+1) = a^{(a \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} k)}$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
For instance, $3 \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} 2 = 3^3 = 27$, which leads to $3 \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} 3 = 3^{27} = 7625597484987$ and $3 \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} 4$ approximating to $10^{3.6383346400240996 \cdot 10^{12}}$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Given this, your task is to determine the last $8$ digits of $1777 \mathbin{\uparrow \uparrow} 1855$, requiring you to apply the principles of hyperexponentiation and modular arithmetic.&lt;/p&gt; In your response, the letter z should appear less than 10 times.

